WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.453
Why divide by the square root of the head size.

2
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:08.195
Suppose the components of q and k are independent with mean 0 and variance 1.

3
00:00:08.475 --> 00:00:09.328
Then q.

4
00:00:09.608 --> 00:00:17.139
k is a sum of d independent products, each with mean 0 and variance 1, so it has mean 0 and variance d.

5
00:00:17.419 --> 00:00:24.203
That’s the argument in footnote 4 of “Attention Is All You Need” (Vaswani and colleagues, 2017).

6
00:00:24.483 --> 00:00:28.920
For a head size of 64, raw scores have a standard deviation of 8.

7
00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:37.456
Feed scores like that into a softmax and it saturates: the largest score wins by a wide margin and the weights are nearly one-hot.

8
00:00:37.736 --> 00:00:38.973
Two things go wrong.

9
00:00:39.253 --> 00:00:44.992
Each token attends to essentially one other token from the very start, before it has learned anything.

10
00:00:45.272 --> 00:00:55.725
And the softmax’s Jacobian, diag(p) minus p p transpose, is nearly zero when p is nearly one-hot, so gradients through the attention weights vanish.

11
00:00:56.005 --> 00:01:06.672
Dividing by the square root of d restores unit variance and keeps the softmax in its responsive range (lecture note 6, at 01:16:56).

12
00:01:06.952 --> 00:01:13.501
This is the same variance bookkeeping as Kaiming initialization in lesson 5, applied to a different product.

13
00:01:13.781 --> 00:01:23.936
Once you see deep learning as a long chain of operations whose scales must be kept near 1, a surprising amount of transformer design reads as variance control.
